# const counts = JSON.parse("[0,5,2]");
# print(raise(counts, 3).join(), counts.join());
# -- end of source
# The loop inside is built twice. Its closed copy is entered first and believes what is known of `raised` in front. In a turn of the open copy the walk forgets that. At the back edge
# of the open copy every fresh object was made to leave for it, and the arm behind the store then arrived at the closed copy against the belief: no try names that entry, the walk failed, and because the edge brings
# no argument nobody looked: the compiler died in Code::endWithJump(). The loops do not name `raised` and it has not left: nothing in them can have written it: what was believed still holds.
# RUN: run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# CHECK: 3 3,6,3
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(COUNT): compile LoopsBuiltTwice PurchasesOfLoopsThatFailed mechanisms=all
# COUNT: statistic LoopsBuiltTwice 4
# COUNT: statistic PurchasesOfLoopsThatFailed 0
